4. What the Windows downloads carry besides Alnasl

   None of this is in the repository; the release workflow puts it in the
   installer and the zip.

   - Erlang/OTP's runtime (Apache-2.0) and the compiled dependencies listed
     in mix.lock, each under its own licence.
   - Velopack (https://github.com/velopack/velopack), MIT licence,
     Copyright (c) Velopack Ltd. and contributors: the installer and
     updater (Setup.exe, Update.exe, the stub beside them) and
     velopack_libc.dll, which the launcher loads to apply an update.
     rel/windows/launcher.c declares the part of its C interface it calls.
   - Microsoft's Visual C++ runtime (vcruntime140.dll, vcruntime140_1.dll,
     msvcp140.dll), which Erlang's Windows build needs, redistributed
     unchanged beside it as Microsoft's terms for those files allow.

5. Stockfish, for fair-play screening

   https://stockfishchess.org, the Stockfish developers, GPL-3.0. Alnasl
   does not contain it and does not distribute it: the owner of a hall has
   Alnasl download the official release (or carries the same archive in on
   a USB stick), after which Alnasl runs it as a separate program over the
   UCI protocol. The fair-play model parameters, once there are any, are
   derived from Lichess broadcast games (database.lichess.org, CC BY-SA
   4.0) and carry that licence.

6. Test games

   priv/games/classics.pgn and the three single games beside it are the
   moves of chess games played between 1620 and 1928. The moves of a chess
   game are facts and are not subject to copyright. The scores were written
   out from memory and checked for legality move by move; they have not
   been compared with a printed source. priv/games/constructed.pgn was
   generated for this project (random legal moves) to cover underpromotion
   and en passant.

